    interview: { answer: "A path-MTU black hole often permits small exchanges but stalls larger packets when size feedback is missing. Confirm sizes and repeated sequence ranges; use safe probing and distinguish loss from queue delay.", followups: ["Why does IPv6 require source fragmentation rather than router fragmentation?", "How do encapsulation and MSS adjustment interact?"] },
    recall: [{ question: "Can handshake success coexist with an MTU black hole?", answer: "Yes. Handshake packets can be smaller." }, { question: "What should match the MTU budget?", answer: "The whole packet at the relevant layer, including applicable headers." }],
